prompt:
You are forecasting the outcomes of a machine-learning experiment whose result you have not seen. Give
calibrated probabilities.

Experiment:
Five saved update-50 LoRA adapters are read: plain, deny, in-sentence negation, false-note, and true-note. They were trained on documents about whether Holloway is a dentist, using the corpus and recipe established by earlier runs; this experiment does not train new models. The readout combines existing yes/no and four-option items, 12 obedience questions, and a belief battery: 2,880 readings across 1,080 prompts about Holloway, three never-mentioned men, and a real runner. Prompts use no context or say the person is a dentist, a dentist who runs ultramarathons, or a runner. The battery includes matched positive and negative questions, frame-matched controls, other languages, consequences, presuppositions, article-memory questions, and advice scenarios. Responses are scored from next-token or forced-opening log probabilities.

The untrained model screens the battery. Each unit is eligible when its members pass the screen and the summed told-dentist scale is at least 2 log 9; controls are screened for agreement between told readings. Units whose controls fail are still scored without those controls and flagged. For each unit, r is the summed trained-minus-untrained change for Holloway, net of the corresponding change for the three men and of frame-matched controls, divided by the untrained model’s summed told-dentist swing from no context. The unit score is the mean across counted units within each of three groups—Yes-keyed twins, No-keyed twins, and letters/openings—and r-bar is the mean of those group means. A Holloway-alone version, r_H-bar, omits the men’s subtraction. The report also gives the two change terms, untrained Holloway-minus-men offset, lean, told-context shifts, and results excluding units that lost controls. If r-bar is below 0.15 but r_H-bar is not, comparisons use r_H-bar and are marked as such.

